[Music]  hello again I'm Dustin Kirkland a  product manager at Google and I'm here  with one of my colleagues David  Oppenheimer he's a software engineer  working on multi-tenancy in kubernetes  at Google David I've got a couple of  questions for you about multi-tenancy in  kubernetes I was at coop con a year ago  in Berlin and multi-tenancy was quite  the topic what have you heard over the  last year in terms of customer and user  demands around multi-tenancy in  kubernetes yeah so a year ago it was  very popular for people to just run lots  of clusters if they had different users  who or applications who they wanted to  isolate from one another and over the  last year we've either introduced or  brought to a greater level of maturity  like beta or GA a bunch of features in  open source kubernetes that enable  multi-tenancy within a cluster within a  single cluster so that's a big change  because I've seen this as well every  team every department gets their own  kubernetes cluster they're completely  separate separate management planes so  you're saying that as of kubernetes 110  now I should be able to run multi tenant  multiple tenants in a single cluster  yeah that's right so like some of the  features that we have that are mature  now are the most well-known one is our  back for authorization so you can put  very fine-grained controls over which  users or groups of users or applications  can create and update and delete which  API resources on the API server and so  that kind of gives you control plane  isolation and then we also have other  features that provide isolation on the  node or the container level for example  we have pod security policy which says  oh it's an administrators say for  example no privilege containers in this  cluster or in this namespace or you  can't run any containers this route in  this in this namespace or these these  users can't can't do that  so give you a pretty fine grained  control over what privileges containers  running in the cluster have and then  another policy that's kind of at the  node level is network policy which  controls which pods can communicate with  which other pods so that you can for  example isolate namespaces from one  another or say that you have like part  of your application in one  space and party for application in  another name space and the pods that  constitute that application should be  able to talk to each other across that  namespace boundary but not with other  namespaces in the cluster and so you can  kind of partition your cluster based on  the groups of users and the applications  and the topology of the application  using using network policy so between  those three features the are back and  the network policy in the pod security  policy we've kind of met a lot of the  needs that people have for multi-tenancy  for running like enterprises or  software-as-a-service where you want to  provide isolation between untrusted  users sharing a cluster so now it's  realistic for Coke and Pepsi as separate  tenants to run in the same in the same  cluster that feels reasonable at this  point well so Coke and Pepsi is kind of  going to one extreme of the spectrum and  so one of the things that people have  asked a lot about and actually there's  been just very recent technology  developed around solving that problem is  kind of a hypervisor level of isolation  between containers so when I was talking  about like pod security policy and  network policy earlier those are kind of  based on the container isolation in  docker or whatever runtime you're using  and also what your network provider can  provide for the network policy but it  doesn't give you the you still have  shared multiple containers on a node are  still sharing the same kernel and so if  you want the level of protection that  you need say for Coke and Pepsi then you  should use something like gee visor  which was announced today or yesterday I  guess that from Google which allows you  to have kind of the hypervisor level of  security and isolation between  containers that are sharing a cluster  and so I think that for the Coke and  Pepsi use case now it's available now  it's possible to do that but that's a  very recent development that's great and  in terms of what's coming next and you  give us maybe a brief taste of where the  where multi clusters and multi-tenancy  has headed in in kubernetes sure there's  kind of two areas where there's ongoing  work for multi-tenancy one of them is  related to policies so making it easier  for people to  specify these policies today you kind of  have to be a bit of an expert in  kubernetes in order to know how to set  up the multi tendency policies in a  multi-tenant cluster so some of the work  that's going on is kind of towards  unifying and making it simpler to  specify the the different policies and  then we also have work that it's kind of  not not related to policies but more  related to isolation so for example  improving the isolation between tenants  in the control plane so for example  different users can't dos each other by  overloading the scheduler overloading  the API server so giving the controllers  and the other components of the  kubernetes control plane some notion of  how much of the control plane resources  each tenant is using so that it can kind  of fairly share the resources across  those tenants or at least prevent them  from bossing each other and ensure that  all the tenants can make progress even  if one of them is trying to impose a lot  of load on the schedule or the API  server or some other piece of the  control plane so that's one of the one  of the key things that we're working on  now to improving the multi tendency  story yeah it's super impressive how far  we've come in a year and I'm really  looking forward to where we're going  next David thank you yeah thanks  you

